/*
	RMSTEST 16 — border percent to tiles, WITHOUT the beach confound.

	RMSTEST_15 was unreadable for a reason I built into it. Its base terrain was
	WATER, and DE auto-generates a BEACH ring wherever land meets water — 432
	beach tiles in that run, a one-tile band all the way around the land. So the
	land's true edge sat one tile outside the grass, and "is the boundary at the
	grass or at the beach" changes every inset by one, which is exactly the
	precision the question needs. The four candidate models differ by one tile.

	Fix: no water anywhere. DESERT base, GRASS land. Nothing triggers beach, so
	the grass edge IS the land edge, full stop.

	SHAPE — READ THE MIDDLE, NOT THE CORNERS. Bordered lands come out chamfered
	(the well-known "borders make a hexagon" behaviour); RMSTEST_15 showed the
	corners cut at 45 degrees over about four tiles. The bounding box still
	reports the correct flat-edge extent, because the chamfer only cuts inward
	from the corners — verified in that run, where the middle row spanned
	exactly the same x range as the bbox. So --bbox remains the right tool. If
	the numbers ever look suspect, re-check against a middle row.

	FOUR EDGES, FOUR VALUES, chosen so every candidate gives a unique signature.
	On TINY (120 wide):

	  left   3  -> 3.6   floor 3 | round 4 | ceil 4
	  right  2  -> 2.4   floor 2 | round 2 | ceil 3
	  top    6  -> 7.2   floor 7 | round 7 | ceil 8
	  bottom 9  -> 10.8  floor 10 | round 11 | ceil 11

	READ IT WITH THE PROBE
	  python probe_scenario.py <scenario> --bbox GRASS

	  left=3 right=3 top=7 bottom=11  -> floor low edges, ceil high edges.
	                                     Sec.4 CONFIRMED as written.
	  left=3 right=2 top=7 bottom=10  -> plain floor on all four.
	  left=4 right=2 top=7 bottom=11  -> plain round-half-up on all four.
	  left=4 right=3 top=8 bottom=11  -> plain ceil on all four.
	  anything else                   -> report all four; the values were picked
	                                     so no two models collide, so an
	                                     unlisted signature means the rule is
	                                     none of these.

	ALSO CHECK, and report even if it looks fine:
	  1. Total tiles. GRASS + DESERT should sum to 14400 with NO third terrain.
	     If a beach or anything else appears, the confound is not gone and the
	     insets are again off by one.
	  2. Axis mapping. All four declared values differ, so if the insets come
	     back transposed then Sec.4's left/right-are-x, top/bottom-are-y claim
	     is wrong.
	  3. border_fuzziness 100 giving a crisp edge, which is Sec.4's reading of
	     the argument as ADHERENCE (guide:903 names it %BorderAdherence). A
	     ragged edge means that reading is inverted, and Sec.6.1's border model
	     needs revisiting across the 19 corpus maps that set it.

	DESIGN NOTES
	  Player lands are DESERT — the same as the base terrain — so they cannot
	  add or remove GRASS anywhere and cannot affect the bbox. circle_radius 5
	  keeps them near the centre, far from every border.
	  land_percent 100 so growth is stopped by the borders, not by a tile
	  budget. base_size 1 and land_position 50 50 so it starts small and central
	  and expands into all four borders evenly.

	RUN ON: TINY, 2 players. The arithmetic above is dim-120 only — on any other
	size this header's predictions are wrong.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
base_terrain DESERT

create_player_lands
{
	terrain_type DESERT
	base_size 3
	number_of_tiles 1
	circle_radius 5 0
}

create_land
{
	terrain_type GRASS
	land_position 50 50
	base_size 1
	land_percent 100
	border_fuzziness 100
	left_border 3
	right_border 2
	top_border 6
	bottom_border 9
}
